You would still be down five points of damage off of each weapon (-2
weapon skill, -3 twoweapon skill) and you would be at -13 to hit for
skill (-4 weapon skill, -9 twoweapon skill) so there is a tiny chance
to mis, more if you are trapped by a beartrap or pit.

The endgame is a cakewalk, but that's because too many of the elements
in the game are linear and additive.

Now that the intel FP bugs are fixed, why not take advantage of
floating point math by replacing a lot of formulas in the form of:

d20 vs target +attacking factors -defending factors

with stuff more like:

Random floating point between 0.0 and 1.0 less than
attacking_factor / (attacking_factor + defending_factor)

So a 30 attacking a 10 (adjusting for all of this AD&D AC10 nonsense)
would be a 75% chance of success rather than 100%.

